 "stop": "20 lines",
 "poem": "On the isle of Sarsunya the\nVenus sits in splendor; behold,\nIn her own narrow chamber\nThe three daughters sit beside her,\nAnd mingle their voices at even.\nAlas, who could have dreamed before\nTheir flight was planned for this hour!\nThou that dost smile on every face,\nDost greet with all thy friendship each\nClose companion to-morrow! Ah,\nI know not if thee I can forget;\nFor once thou lovedst Venus!\nSince thou hast known her smiles and faces,\nEre to my heart's good-natured eyes\nA shadow fell a moment ago,\nLet me remember, remembering,\nWhat she said when we parted.\nSo saying it I sat him waiting,\nAnd though he called me 'thee' again\nBy the same name long since in heaven,",
